  "body": {
    "id": "prop:binary-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "When \\(K=2\\), projection of \\(\\mathcal R(P)\\) onto \\(q(1)\\) equals the sharp interval \\([V^-(P),V^+(P)]\\) from Shahn2026Oracle, so the construction introduces no extra restriction in the binary-outcome special case.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "K",
      "\\mathcal R(P)",
      "P",
      "q",
      "V^-(P)",
      "V^+(P)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:tree-polytope",
      "thm:tree-projection-lift",
      "lem:shahn-binary-oracle-sharpness"
    ],
    "justification": "The reduction is the required sanity check against the known sharp binary recursion.",
    "gap": "This is a compatibility corollary rather than a novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "Existing binary oracle analyses can adopt the full-law machinery without changing their established endpoints.",
    "proof_tex": "When \\(K=2\\), a mass function \\(q\\) is uniquely determined by \\(q(1)\\), since \\(q(0)=1-q(1)\\).  The projection-and-lift theorem gives\n\\[\n \\operatorname{proj}_{q(1)}\\mathcal R(P)\n =\\{Q(M_Q=1):Q\\in\\mathfrak F(P)\\}.                       \\tag{35}\n\\]\nThe cited sharp binary oracle theorem identifies the right side of (35) as \\([V^-(P),V^+(P)]\\).  Hence the full-law formulation has exactly the established binary interval and imposes no additional restriction.",
